			I've been lurking on this thread, and I'm struck by how this debate about 'cool' versus 'interesting' is really a scoping issue. In standup, engineers tell me a feature is 'interesting' when they want to rewrite the architecture. In the stakeholder demo, they call it 'cool' when the spinner doesn't spin forever. Meanwhile, I'm sitting there thinking, 'Can we take this offline and align on scope?' Because at the end of the day, the roadmap is a lie, the velocity is a fiction, and the only thing that's 'correct' is the release date I promised to a VP who doesn't understand why we can't just 'add a button'.

			But let me steelman the art metaphor, because it's the most honest thing here. Broken code is absolutely modern art — it's a commentary on the futility of estimation and the impermanence of requirements. It hangs in the gallery of our ticket tracker, and we curate it every sprint during grooming. The problem is that business stakeholders don't want to buy the art; they want to know if it's load-bearing. So I translate: 'Interesting' is what we say when we haven't figured out the edge cases. 'Cool' is what we say after the demo works once on a good internet connection. 'Correct' is what we say in the postmortem, right before we apologize for the outage.

			Here's the real kicker: the builders care about interesting because they're the ones who have to live with the code. I care about whether it moves a metric or, at minimum, doesn't get me another 2 a.m. tweet from a customer. So yes, software engineering is interesting — like a puzzle designed by a sadist who keeps changing the rules mid-game. But if we're aligning on outcomes, I'd rather have boring, correct, and shipped than fascinating and perpetually in 'one more sprint.' That's the vibe that matters in my world. Now let's circle back on the roadmap — it needs more buffer, and by 'buffer' I mean 'three weeks of chaos we'll pretend we planned.'
